package com.hpcai270

// Days 16–50: foundation-to-admin bridge. Detailed chapters can replace these later.
val hpcDaysPart4 = listOf(
Day(16,"Linux for HPC","The operating system","Linux is the standard operating system for HPC infrastructure. Storage, networking, schedulers and GPU software are administered from Linux.","KEY AREAS\n\nProcesses\nMemory\nFilesystems\nDevices\nNetworking\nServices\nLogs\nPermissions","List five Linux areas an HPC administrator must understand."),
Day(17,"Linux filesystem","Paths, mounts and devices","Linux exposes storage through a filesystem tree. A mounted filesystem connects a storage resource to a directory in that tree.","/ → root\n/home → users\n/mnt → mounts\n/dev → devices\n/proc → process/kernel information\n\nStorage troubleshooting starts by understanding what is mounted.","Explain the difference between a device and a mounted filesystem."),
Day(18,"Linux processes","What is running?","A process is a running program. HPC administrators inspect processes to understand resource use, hangs and unexpected workload behavior.","COMMON TOOLS\n\nps → process snapshot\ntop → live view\nhtop → interactive view\nkill → signal a process\n\nCPU and memory pressure can affect storage and network performance.","Use ps and top on a Linux machine and identify one process."),
Day(19,"Linux memory","RAM, cache and pressure","Memory is working space for applications and the operating system. Linux also uses memory for filesystem cache, which can change observed I/O behavior.","APPLICATION\n ↓\nRAM / PAGE CACHE\n ↓\nSTORAGE\n\nDo not assume cached I/O represents storage-device performance.","Why can the same fio test behave differently after cache effects?"),
Day(20,"Linux permissions","Users, groups and access","HPC systems are shared environments. Files and directories need correct ownership and permissions so users and services can access only what they should.","OWNER + GROUP + MODE\n\nread → r\nwrite → w\nexecute → x\n\nPermissions are part of storage troubleshooting.","Explain rwx for a file and a directory."),
Day(21,"Linux networking","Interfaces and routes","HPC administrators need to understand interfaces, IP addresses, routes and connectivity before troubleshooting high-speed fabrics.","HOST\n ↓\nNIC → IP → ROUTE → NETWORK\n\nUseful commands: ip addr, ip route, ping, ss.","Run ip addr and identify the active interface."),
Day(22,"Linux logs","Evidence before guessing","Logs provide evidence about services, devices, filesystems and network problems. Good administrators correlate timestamps across systems.","EVENT\n ↓\nKERNEL / SERVICE LOG\n ↓\nTIMELINE\n ↓\nROOT CAUSE","Find one Linux log source and explain what it records."),
Day(23,"Shell basics","Bash as an administrator tool","Bash lets administrators automate repetitive tasks and combine commands into repeatable workflows.","COMMAND → PIPE → FILTER → OUTPUT\n\nExamples: ls, grep, awk, sed, sort, uniq, tee.","Write a command pipeline that filters a log for a keyword."),
Day(24,"Bash scripting","Repeatable operations","A shell script turns a sequence of commands into a reusable operational procedure. Error handling and clear output matter.","SCRIPT\n ↓\nINPUT\n ↓\nCHECK\n ↓\nACTION\n ↓\nREPORT\n\nAutomation should be predictable and reversible.","Write a small script that checks whether a mount exists."),
Day(25,"Python basics","Automation beyond Bash","Python is useful when automation needs structured data, APIs, parsing, logic or larger workflows.","PYTHON\n\nvariables\nconditions\nloops\nfunctions\nfiles\nJSON\n\nYou do not need advanced software development to automate infrastructure.","Write a Python program that prints three storage device names."),
Day(26,"Storage protocols","Block, file and object","Storage can be exposed as block, file or object. Each model presents data differently and fits different workloads.","BLOCK → volume / device\nFILE → filesystem / path\nOBJECT → object + metadata + API\n\nExamples: FC/iSCSI, NFS/SMB, S3.","Give one HPC or AI use case for each model."),
Day(27,"NFS","Shared file storage","NFS lets Linux clients access files from a remote server. It is important background knowledge even when moving toward parallel filesystems.","CLIENT → NETWORK → NFS SERVER → STORAGE\n\nUnderstand exports, mounts, permissions and network dependencies.","Draw an NFS client-to-server path."),
Day(28,"S3","Object storage","S3 is an object-storage API model. Objects are addressed through buckets and keys rather than traditional filesystem paths.","APPLICATION\n ↓ HTTPS / S3 API\nBUCKET\n ↓\nOBJECT + METADATA\n\nCloudian and many AI data platforms expose S3-compatible interfaces.","Explain bucket, object and key."),
Day(29,"RAID concepts","Protection versus performance","RAID combines drives to improve availability, capacity or performance. HPC architectures may use different protection strategies depending on the layer.","RAID 0 → performance / no redundancy\nRAID 1 → mirror\nRAID 5/6 → parity\nRAID 10 → mirror + stripe","Explain why redundancy and performance are different design goals."),
Day(30,"Storage performance","IOPS, throughput and latency","Storage performance has multiple dimensions. A system can have high throughput but poor small-I/O latency, or high IOPS but limited sequential bandwidth.","LATENCY → time per I/O\nIOPS → I/O operations per second\nTHROUGHPUT → data per second\n\nAlways identify workload and I/O size.","Define IOPS, throughput and latency in your own words."),
Day(31,"FIO","The storage benchmark","fio is a flexible I/O workload generator used to measure storage behavior under controlled workloads.","IMPORTANT VARIABLES\n\nread/write\nblock size\nqueue depth\nthreads/jobs\ndirect I/O\nruntime\n\nA benchmark is meaningful only when its workload is understood.","Learn what block size and queue depth change in a test."),
Day(32,"Queue depth","How much I/O is outstanding?","Queue depth describes how many I/O operations can be outstanding. Increasing it can improve device utilization until another bottleneck appears.","LOW QD → fewer outstanding requests\nHIGH QD → more concurrency\n\nMore is not automatically better.","Explain why maximum queue depth can eventually stop improving performance."),
Day(33,"Latency","The hidden performance metric","Latency is the time required to complete an operation. In distributed systems, latency accumulates across storage, network and software layers.","APPLICATION\n ↓ latency\nFILESYSTEM\n ↓\nNETWORK\n ↓\nSTORAGE","Name three places latency can be introduced."),
Day(34,"Throughput","Moving data at scale","Throughput measures how much data moves per unit time. Parallel workloads often need aggregate throughput rather than single-client throughput.","1 CLIENT → X GB/s\n100 CLIENTS → aggregate workload\n\nThe system must scale without creating a bottleneck.","Explain the difference between single-client and aggregate throughput."),
Day(35,"HPC storage architecture","Putting storage together","HPC storage separates client access, metadata and data services so many compute nodes can operate concurrently.","CLIENTS\n ↓\nPARALLEL FILESYSTEM\n ↓\nMETADATA + DATA SERVICES\n ↓\nSTORAGE MEDIA\n\nThe architecture is designed around concurrency.","Draw a simple parallel filesystem architecture."),
Day(36,"Lustre architecture","MDS, MDT, OSS and OST","Lustre separates metadata services from bulk file data. Understanding these roles is essential for administering and troubleshooting Lustre.","CLIENT → MDS/MDT → metadata\nCLIENT → OSS/OST → file data\n\nThe terms describe service and storage roles.","Explain MDS, MDT, OSS and OST."),
Day(37,"Lustre clients","Mounting and access","A Lustre client mounts the filesystem and interacts with metadata and object storage services as required by file operations.","USER APPLICATION\n ↓\nLUSTRE CLIENT\n ↙       ↘\nMETADATA   DATA","Explain why a client may contact different Lustre services for one file operation."),
Day(38,"Lustre striping","Parallel file placement","Lustre can stripe file data across multiple OSTs. This allows large sequential workloads to use multiple storage targets concurrently.","FILE\n ↙ ↓ ↘\nOST1 OST2 OST3\n\nStripe count and stripe size affect workload behavior.","Explain striping using a three-disk example."),
Day(39,"Lustre troubleshooting","Think in layers","Troubleshooting starts with symptoms and works downward through client, network, metadata, storage targets and media.","SYMPTOM\n ↓\nCLIENT\n ↓\nNETWORK\n ↓\nLUSTRE SERVICES\n ↓\nSTORAGE\n\nMeasure before changing configuration.","Create a five-step Lustre troubleshooting checklist."),
Day(40,"GPFS / Storage Scale","Another parallel filesystem","IBM Storage Scale evolved from GPFS and is widely used for scalable file access, including HPC and AI workloads.","CLIENTS\n ↓\nSTORAGE SCALE\n ↓\nSHARED DATA\n\nCompare its purpose with Lustre rather than memorizing product names.","Give two similarities between Lustre and Storage Scale."),
Day(41,"BeeGFS","Parallel filesystem alternative","BeeGFS is another parallel filesystem used in HPC environments. It provides a useful comparison when learning distributed file architectures.","CLIENTS → MANAGEMENT / METADATA / STORAGE SERVICES\n\nDifferent products use different terminology for similar architectural ideas.","Why should an administrator learn architecture instead of only product commands?"),
Day(42,"Ceph","Software-defined distributed storage","Ceph distributes storage across commodity servers and can provide object, block and file services through different components.","CEPH\n\nMON → cluster state\nOSD → data storage\nMGR → management\nRADOS → distributed data layer","Explain why Ceph is broader than a filesystem."),
Day(43,"NVMe deeper","PCIe and parallel queues","NVMe is designed for flash and modern non-volatile media. Its command model supports substantial parallelism and low software overhead.","CPU → PCIe → NVMe controller → media\n\nUnderstand namespaces, controllers and queues at a high level.","Explain the path from a host CPU to an NVMe device."),
Day(44,"NVMe-oF transports","Remote NVMe","NVMe over Fabrics extends NVMe semantics across a network fabric. Transport choices include TCP, RDMA and Fibre Channel.","HOST → NVMe-oF → TARGET\n\nTransport affects latency, networking requirements and operational design.","Compare NVMe/TCP and NVMe/RDMA at a high level."),
Day(45,"RDMA fundamentals","Bypassing traditional paths","RDMA enables direct data movement between systems with reduced CPU involvement in parts of the data path.","APPLICATION\n ↓\nRDMA STACK\n ↓\nNIC ===== NIC\n\nThe goal is efficient communication for demanding distributed workloads.","Explain why CPU overhead matters in large HPC clusters."),
Day(46,"InfiniBand","HPC networking fabric","InfiniBand is a high-performance fabric widely used in HPC and AI clusters. It provides the communication substrate for compute, storage and collective operations.","GPU NODE → HCA → IB SWITCH → HCA → STORAGE / GPU NODE\n\nLearn links, ports, subnets and fabric concepts next.","Identify the role of the HCA and switch."),
Day(47,"RoCEv2","RDMA over Ethernet","RoCEv2 carries RDMA traffic over an Ethernet/IP network. Correct congestion and loss management become important for reliable high-performance operation.","RDMA\n ↓\nRoCEv2\n ↓\nETHERNET / IP\n\nAI clusters can use Ethernet fabrics when designed for RDMA workloads.","Explain what RoCEv2 adds to ordinary Ethernet networking."),
Day(48,"AI storage pipeline","Keeping GPUs supplied","AI training repeatedly reads datasets, feeds GPU memory and writes checkpoints. Storage and networking can directly affect GPU utilization.","DATASET → STORAGE → NETWORK → GPU MEMORY → GPU\n                         ↑\n                    CHECKPOINTS","Name two ways storage can become an AI training bottleneck."),
Day(49,"NCCL and collective I/O","Distributed AI communication","NCCL provides optimized collective communication for NVIDIA GPUs. Storage is one part of a larger AI infrastructure path involving GPU memory and high-speed networking.","GPU 1 ↔ GPU 2 ↔ GPU 3 ↔ GPU 4\n\nAI performance depends on coordination across compute, network and storage layers.","Explain why storage performance alone does not determine AI training performance."),
Day(50,"50-day checkpoint","Your HPC-AI foundation","After 50 days you should understand the vocabulary and architecture connecting enterprise storage to HPC and AI infrastructure.","YOUR MAP\n\nLINUX → NETWORK → STORAGE → LUSTRE / SCALE → NVMe → RDMA → IB / RoCE → GPU → NCCL\n\nNext phase: deeper administration, labs, benchmarking and troubleshooting.","Explain the complete map aloud in five minutes without notes.")
)
